lesson.body:
Defining a function in Python always follows the same shape: the keyword def, then a name you choose, then parentheses that may contain the names of values the function needs, then a colon, then an indented block containing the statements that make up the function's body. Writing def square(n): return n * n creates a function named square that expects one value, refers to it inside the body as n, and produces that value multiplied by itself. Every part of this pattern is required. Leaving out the colon, forgetting to indent the body, or misspelling def all produce errors, because Python's parser depends on this exact structure to recognize where a function starts and where it ends.

One of the most important facts about def is easy to miss: running a def statement does not execute the code inside it. It only creates the function and attaches it to the name you gave it, the way an assignment statement attaches a value to a variable. If you type def square(n): return n * n into a Python session by itself, nothing appears to happen, no calculation runs, no output prints. The body only executes later, and only when the function is actually called with square(some_number). This separation between defining and calling is the same separation between writing a recipe and cooking a meal from it: writing the recipe does not put food on the table, and defining a function does not, by itself, produce a result.

Tracing through a slightly larger example makes the structure concrete. Suppose you write:

def describe_temp(celsius):
    if celsius > 30:
        return "hot"
    elif celsius > 15:
        return "mild"
    else:
        return "cold"

This defines describe_temp with one expected value, celsius. Nothing runs yet. Later, when the program calls describe_temp(22), Python jumps into the body with celsius bound to 22. The condition celsius > 30 checks 22 > 30, which is False, so control moves to the elif. celsius > 15 checks 22 > 15, which is True, so the function returns "mild" and execution jumps back out to wherever describe_temp(22) was called, carrying that string with it.

The name you give a function in its def line matters beyond just labeling it. Python uses that name to look up the function every time you call it, so def square(n): ... and a later call to square(5) only work together because the names match exactly, including capitalization. This connects back to why functions exist in the first place: a well-chosen name like describe_temp or square documents, right at the call site, what the code is meant to do, without forcing whoever reads it to open the definition and read through the body just to understand its purpose.

Function names follow the same identifier rules as variable names: they must start with a letter or underscore, contain only letters, digits, and underscores after that, and cannot be one of Python's reserved words like def or return. Convention in Python, laid out in the language's official style guide, favors lowercase words separated by underscores, such as describe_temp rather than DescribeTemp or describeTemp, so that functions are visually easy to tell apart from class names, which conventionally use a different capitalization style. None of this affects whether the code runs, since Python does not enforce naming style, but consistent naming makes a program dramatically easier for you, or anyone else, to read months later.
